  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 326 PM CDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\nOn the heels of the exiting system Monday, a northern stream \ndisturbance will pass to the north of the forecast area over the\nDakotas. A second disturbance will track from Kansas into SE \nNebraska. This will bring a chance of showers to far \nsoutheastern portions of the forecast area Tuesday evening. \nClouds and fog are anticipated Tuesday morning across the area. \nBy afternoon locations in the western portion of the forecast \narea will see some west or southwest winds which will lead to \nclearing skies Tuesday afternoon. Further east, skies should \nremain mostly cloudy and will have an impact on highs. Readings \nwill range from the upper 50s over eastern portions of the \nforecast area with highs in the middle 60s in the west and \nnorthwest. A low amplitude pattern will commence across the \nwestern CONUS midweek. This will lead to mainly dry conditions \nand warmer than normal temperatures for the high plains. The \npattern will begin to amplify Thursday night into Friday as low \npressure deepens over the Pacific NW. Downstream of this \nfeature, a southern stream disturbance will lift from the SW \nCONUS into the southern plains. As this system crosses into \nKansas and Oklahoma Friday, there will be an increased threat \nfor precipitation mainly over the eastern half of Nebraska. \nTemperatures will be mild for Wednesday through Sunday with \nhighs generally in the 60s.",
